So, the optimal interval covering $p_1$ should start at some index $l$ such that $l \le p_1 \le l+k-1$. To cover the most to the right, we want $l+k-1$ to be as large as possible. The constraint is that the interval must cover $p_1$, so $l \le p_1$. The maximum right endpoint is $p_1 + k - 1$. Thus, we should pick the interval $[p_1 - (k-1), p_1]$? No, wait.
The interval is defined by its start position or end position. Let's say we pick an interval $[i, i+k-1]$. This interval covers $p_1$ if $i \le p_1 \le i+k-1$.
To cover $p_1$ and extend as far right as possible, we should set the right endpoint to be $p_1 + k - 1$. The start position would be $p_1$. Wait, if the interval is $[p_1, p_1+k-1]$, it covers $p_1$. If we shift it left, say $[p_1-1, p_1+k-2]$, it still covers $p_1$ but ends earlier. So, to cover $p_1$ and maximize the reach to the right, the interval should be $[p_1, p_1+k-1]$.
